1
00:00:00,160 --> 00:00:02,080
Welcome back to Google Deep Mind the podcast.

2
00:00:02,160 --> 00:00:03,360
I'm Professor Hannah Fry.

3
00:00:03,854 --> 00:00:04,560
Picture the scene.

4
00:00:04,720 --> 00:00:15,940
You are scrolling through your socials and you see some remarkable footage, the up-close, slow-motion eruption of a volcano or a tornado raging through a village, or perhaps some footage of a protest.

5
00:00:16,239 --> 00:00:18,596
I think many of us ask the same question.

6
00:00:18,926 --> 00:00:19,600
Is that real?

7
00:00:19,760 --> 00:00:22,320
Was this made by a human or a machine?

8
00:00:22,480 --> 00:00:31,200
Well, as AI gets better at generating text and images and audio and video, that is becoming one of the most challenging questions of our time.

9
00:00:31,440 --> 00:00:34,880
Except that now the answer just got a lot more complicated.

10
00:00:35,246 --> 00:00:45,440
We are entering a world where AI can design biological molecules and structures that have never existed in nature before, some of which could be designed to cause harm.

11
00:00:45,680 --> 00:00:50,160
So how do you stop something dangerous before it gets made?

12
00:00:50,400 --> 00:01:00,880
Well, the answer, it turns out, might be hidden in plain sight, embedded invisibly into the very thing you're looking at, or the very molecule you're about to synthesize.

13
00:01:01,120 --> 00:01:07,040
A watermark, not the blocky logo you see on a stock photo, something far more subtle, something mathematical.

14
00:01:07,280 --> 00:01:11,360
And today I am joined by two people who are working on this from different angles.

15
00:01:11,600 --> 00:01:21,200
Pushmi Kooli is VP of Science at Google DeepMind and one of the architects of SynthID, the watermarking system now being adopted across the AI industry.

16
00:01:21,440 --> 00:01:27,760
And Jeremy Radcliffe is a biosecurity researcher whose team has applied that same technology to biology.

17
00:01:28,142 --> 00:01:29,520
Welcome to the podcast, both of you.

18
00:01:29,902 --> 00:01:31,040
Jeremy, let's start with you.

19
00:01:31,280 --> 00:01:37,360
Watermarking anything that AI spits out, I mean, it sounds like a very good idea, but just break it down for me.

20
00:01:37,520 --> 00:01:38,640
Why is this helpful?

21
00:01:38,960 --> 00:01:46,800
So for biology, sort of the motivation for being able to watermarking is so that we can have provenance and say specifically where did this sequence come from?

22
00:01:46,880 --> 00:01:54,640
And really what we're trying to differentiate is between sequences that come from things in nature versus sequences that come as products of AI systems.

23
00:01:54,880 --> 00:02:07,040
So specifically, the type of risk that we're thinking about in terms of AI systems is sort of an AI-generated sequence that can have properties that we know to be problematic, but we might not be able to identify from the sequence itself that it has these problematic properties.

24
00:02:07,360 --> 00:02:09,120
And what makes a good watermark?

25
00:02:09,199 --> 00:02:14,724
Because I think when people consider the phrase, they sort of think of, I don't know, like banknotes or like passports.

26
00:02:15,200 --> 00:02:18,160
Is there like rules for what counts as a good watermark?

27
00:02:18,480 --> 00:02:22,080
I think for us, in particular, human imperceptibility is one.

28
00:02:22,254 --> 00:02:26,720
So making it very difficult for someone to be able to tell what portion of data has been watermarked.

29
00:02:26,880 --> 00:02:28,480
Because otherwise it would be really easy to erase.

30
00:02:28,800 --> 00:02:28,960
Yeah.

31
00:02:29,134 --> 00:02:36,160
Having high detectability, so having some system that can go through and actually identify the watermark with high confidence, and then also good generalizability.

32
00:02:36,240 --> 00:02:43,040
So being able to have at least a watermarking method that can work across a bunch of different systems rather than having to make bespoke ones for every single data type.

33
00:02:43,534 --> 00:02:44,320
What do you think, Pushmi?

34
00:02:44,480 --> 00:02:46,800
I mean, does that apply beyond biology and more broadly?

35
00:02:47,040 --> 00:03:04,880
Yeah, I think the main reason why we started the whole watermarking project at DeepMind almost eight years ago now was to give a sense of provenance to what users are seeing.

36
00:03:05,120 --> 00:03:29,156
If you look at different modalities, whether it's text, whether it's images, whether it's videos, it was hard to imagine ten years back that we would today be talking about systems that can create images, videos, audio, text, and proteins, or enzymes that are indistinguishable from what a human or what exists in nature.

37
00:03:29,920 --> 00:03:36,160
And it's an important sort of element for users to give users this idea that you can.

38
00:03:37,056 --> 00:03:39,684
Really believe what you are seeing.

39
00:03:40,270 --> 00:03:42,692
So, what do we need from a watermark?

40
00:03:42,896 --> 00:03:44,336
We need three properties.

41
00:03:44,576 --> 00:03:48,496
One, it should not degrade the quality, otherwise, basically the whole point is lost.

42
00:03:48,750 --> 00:03:49,572
People will not use it.

43
00:03:50,030 --> 00:03:55,616
The second element is robustness against attack or against transformations.

44
00:03:55,790 --> 00:04:01,856
If people want to remove it, it's hard to remove in the sense that even if you make changes to the signal, it will persist.

45
00:04:02,030 --> 00:04:05,776
And then the third requirement, is that it should be easy to use, right?

46
00:04:07,776 --> 00:04:12,656
it should be easy to integrate in the signal and it's easy to detect from the signal.

47
00:04:12,910 --> 00:04:34,896
So, those are the three sort of properties of imperceptibility, robustness, and scalability that we have designed all our watermarking systems for, with the whole idea that users have more control and have a better view of what they are seeing or what they are sort of using or designing and so on.

48
00:04:35,438 --> 00:04:38,736
It feels like this subject of watermarking has been quite a big deal this summer.

49
00:04:39,342 --> 00:04:44,976
But at the same time, we're a few years into generative AI being accessible to the public.

50
00:04:45,056 --> 00:04:47,100
What's taken so long, Prashmi?

51
00:04:47,216 --> 00:04:50,084
why is it only now that this has become so widespread?

52
00:04:50,496 --> 00:05:04,388
When image editing software started becoming very sophisticated, people were sort of very concerned about this issue of is this a real image or has this been tampered with?

53
00:05:04,686 --> 00:05:20,964
And there was a lot of work and specialized systems which could really sort of analyze an image and think and see the tiny differences that exist in a tampered image and can say, oh yeah, this was a fake image or this has been tampered with and so on.

54
00:05:21,646 --> 00:05:27,136
But as generative AI became more sophisticated, those tiny differences went away.

55
00:05:27,854 --> 00:05:40,772
And then that naturally sort of resulted in the question: is this game over in the sense that humans will not have the ability to detect whether something is they are generated?

56
00:05:41,198 --> 00:05:43,216
And this is where watermarking sort of came in.

57
00:05:43,376 --> 00:06:01,380
We said the way to sort of make sure that humans always have the ability to understand the origin of where some signal came from is by injecting a signal within it, having a bias so it doesn't go away.

58
00:06:01,696 --> 00:06:23,652
Even if the models are perfect, and they have the ability to sort of generate images, which are indistinguishable from what a camera would observe, or what a text that a human would write, we specifically sort of put in some imperceptible things that can allow users to later detect that this was, or this originated from our AI model.

59
00:06:24,462 --> 00:06:28,496
And when you say we here, Pushmi, you really do mean we in the literal sense, right?

60
00:06:29,230 --> 00:06:33,296
it was you guys that came up with a solution for this one that was scalable.

61
00:06:33,616 --> 00:06:33,776
Yes.

62
00:06:33,870 --> 00:06:37,296
So our team has been working on this for almost eight years.

63
00:06:37,456 --> 00:06:46,436
We started with images because people were concerned about these images being used for fake news, misinformation, and so on.

64
00:06:46,896 --> 00:06:51,296
And so there was that motivation to solve that problem.

65
00:06:51,536 --> 00:07:07,056
At the same time, although the problem is extremely challenging because of the properties we just mentioned of robustness and imperceptibility and efficiency and scalability, still an image is a very large amount of data.

66
00:07:07,630 --> 00:07:18,628
A one megapixel image has sort of one million sort of numbers or like three million numbers, depending on how it's encoded and so on, to hide information, right?

67
00:07:18,894 --> 00:07:20,480
And without changing the content.

68
00:07:20,720 --> 00:07:25,828
So we developed watermarking systems for images first.

69
00:07:26,160 --> 00:07:39,760
Later on, we had to look at the challenge of how do you do this for other modalities like text, which are not as high-dimensional as images, where actually it's a very small amount of signal, right?

70
00:07:40,640 --> 00:07:40,800
So

71
00:07:40,800 --> 00:07:42,240
a million numbers in a sentence, is there?

72
00:07:42,560 --> 00:07:43,200
Yeah, exactly.

73
00:07:43,374 --> 00:07:44,320
It's a few words.

74
00:07:44,480 --> 00:07:51,812
You can't change those sort of few words, and you have to be very cautious in terms of not changing the meaning of the content.

75
00:07:52,270 --> 00:08:05,120
And so the approaches that we developed for watermarking text were quite different and distinct from what we use for watermarking high-dimensional signals like images and videos and audio, even.

76
00:08:05,600 --> 00:08:09,600
But this idea of watermarking, which you guys pioneered, has now been brought into law.

77
00:08:09,680 --> 00:08:14,756
It's essential that any generative content within the EU has to have some sort of watermarking.

78
00:08:15,120 --> 00:08:27,428
I think what regulators and countries around the world have recognized that they want to give users this information as to the origins of the content that they are consuming.

79
00:08:27,680 --> 00:08:35,039
And one sort of key technology that is seen as a robust solution for it that can scale is watermarking.

80
00:08:35,120 --> 00:08:50,788
And which is why not only have we seen a take up of these, uh, of this idea across the industry, the fact that Synth ID is used by Nvidia, is used by other partners, like with OpenAI and so on.

81
00:08:51,360 --> 00:09:00,480
it's a remarkable sort of thing that many of our partners in the generative AI space have adopted this technology.

82
00:09:00,942 --> 00:09:06,320
And Jeremy, in this episode, we're going to be talking about two different applications of watermarking for AI.

83
00:09:06,400 --> 00:09:13,060
So you've got text and video and audio and so on, and then you've also got for biological structures and sequences.

84
00:09:13,870 --> 00:09:15,840
Is the solution quite similar between the two?

85
00:09:16,360 --> 00:09:18,480
Yes, I would say they're motivated by similar principles.

86
00:09:18,560 --> 00:09:25,760
And we were able to adopt a lot of the work that had already been done, including using the open source synthetic text library and just embedding that directly into another system.

87
00:09:26,000 --> 00:09:26,240
Right.

88
00:09:27,280 --> 00:09:30,880
not just the same tricks, but sometimes the actual same code.

89
00:09:30,960 --> 00:09:32,080
You can sort of pick it up and drop it.

90
00:09:32,320 --> 00:09:35,040
At least start from the same code and then make some adaptations on top of it.

91
00:09:35,760 --> 00:09:36,000
Okay.

92
00:09:36,080 --> 00:09:45,920
so I think people want to sort of understand how this works, because it sounds almost magical that you could have a single sentence and you have embedded some sort of watermark in there.

93
00:09:45,920 --> 00:09:47,200
that's nothing to do with white space.

94
00:09:47,280 --> 00:09:58,320
it's nothing to do with like putting commas in with hiding them in the text, but that the words themselves are hiding a record of how they were generated and where they came from.

95
00:09:58,606 --> 00:10:02,720
So if it's okay with you, Pishmi, I thought we might just walk through how this works for text.

96
00:10:02,800 --> 00:10:04,560
I think that's probably the easiest one to understand.

97
00:10:05,134 --> 00:10:16,560
Okay, so before we get to that, there is some software out there already that you can get online that claims to be able to tell whether the text that you have is generated by AI or not.

98
00:10:16,800 --> 00:10:20,000
How is that different from what you guys have created?

99
00:10:20,240 --> 00:10:37,284
Yeah, so I think the traditional approach that has been there for a long time is to train machine learning models to solve a classification problem, to distinguish between what is generated by a model versus what was captured in the natural world.

100
00:10:37,600 --> 00:10:44,000
And this sort of involves basically training this machine learning models over over a very large amount of data, right?

101
00:10:44,160 --> 00:10:56,880
When the earlier generations of large language models and these conversational agents came about, you could sort of make out that oh, this is a large language model, it has certain tells, it has

102
00:10:56,880 --> 00:10:57,920
it's not X, it's Y.

103
00:10:58,240 --> 00:10:59,040
Yeah, it uses the

104
00:10:59,040 --> 00:11:00,080
word quietly a bit too much.

105
00:11:00,400 --> 00:11:00,880
Yeah, exactly.

106
00:11:01,870 --> 00:11:07,908
but as these models have become more and more sophisticated, those tells have become less frequent.

107
00:11:08,320 --> 00:11:24,160
And so if you have this machine learning classifier which is trying to distinguish the classifier's accuracy goes down over time as the generative AI models become better at replicating what they are targeted to achieve.

108
00:11:24,784 --> 00:11:28,384
Okay, well let's walk through how the watermarking system works then.

109
00:11:28,464 --> 00:11:30,624
So so let's do it sort of step by step if we can.

110
00:11:30,990 --> 00:11:37,984
So first off, if you could maybe we should start off by how large language models pick the next word in a sentence when it's generating text.

111
00:11:38,304 --> 00:11:47,908
Yeah so when we are watermarking text, the idea is that these tokens or words are being generated from a large language model.

112
00:11:48,430 --> 00:11:57,060
And the large language model essentially has a distribution over which tokens it can or which words it can generate.

113
00:11:57,486 --> 00:12:01,540
So if I ask you the question, Hannah, what did you have for breakfast today?

114
00:12:02,064 --> 00:12:09,584
you can start with the word I or you could start with the word push me, the addressing me.

115
00:12:10,254 --> 00:12:12,224
and these are the options that you have.

116
00:12:12,494 --> 00:12:15,716
And if they're equally valid starting points, you have optionality.

117
00:12:16,110 --> 00:12:24,516
And this is where text watermarking leverages this optionality by seeing what does the key tell us.

118
00:12:25,038 --> 00:12:26,704
Okay, so but that's the point, right?

119
00:12:26,784 --> 00:12:36,624
Is that sometimes when you have an option for a word, behind the scenes there is a secret key which says bias these words over others.

120
00:12:37,326 --> 00:12:47,744
And then when you look back at all of the text together, if you can see those words appearing over and over and over again, you can be confident that this is AI generated.

121
00:12:48,064 --> 00:12:51,184
So rather than the words, it's the patterns of those words.

122
00:12:51,504 --> 00:12:59,264
So those words specifically you don't need to be repeated, but the patterns is what the detector sort of leverages.

123
00:12:59,584 --> 00:13:01,264
How they appear in sort of chains together.

124
00:13:01,424 --> 00:13:01,824
Exactly.

125
00:13:02,064 --> 00:13:02,372
Right.

126
00:13:02,544 --> 00:13:03,344
Yes, okay.

127
00:13:03,504 --> 00:13:05,348
So you're secretly, right?

128
00:13:05,904 --> 00:13:19,264
I mean it's not that secret, you're sort of talking about it on a podcast, we're going to say, but you have some code running around in the background, some secret key that you can then look at a chunk of text later and say, hang on a second, this looks like it was generated by AI.

129
00:13:19,584 --> 00:13:31,824
Not only this was generated by AI, but if different AI models are using different keys, we can sort of check whether this was generated by this model or this other model.

130
00:13:32,224 --> 00:13:32,544
Okay.

131
00:13:33,102 --> 00:13:37,664
So you can tell this is Gemini, this is whatever other model might have.

132
00:13:39,104 --> 00:13:39,984
Right, if you have the key.

133
00:13:40,384 --> 00:13:41,188
Okay, I see.

134
00:13:41,504 --> 00:13:46,944
Okay, so so but if there's few words that it could be, then you can't use it.

135
00:13:47,118 --> 00:13:50,384
So does that mean that some text is unwatermarkable?

136
00:13:50,704 --> 00:13:50,944
Yes.

137
00:13:51,024 --> 00:13:57,984
So if you have a very small, a short text snippet, right?

138
00:13:58,064 --> 00:14:00,260
Like what is the capital of France?

139
00:14:01,184 --> 00:14:02,064
it is Paris.

140
00:14:02,144 --> 00:14:05,664
So there is very little that we can change there.

141
00:14:05,984 --> 00:14:09,504
And so that will not be watermarked.

142
00:14:09,664 --> 00:14:12,304
That is a limitation of the watermarking approach in this case.

143
00:14:12,624 --> 00:14:17,504
Does all of this mean then that students can't cheat on their essays anymore, bluntly?

144
00:14:17,664 --> 00:14:22,304
I mean, are we now able to just detect anything that's AI generated?

145
00:14:22,670 --> 00:14:32,932
I think the whole ecosystem as a whole is moving towards watermarking, as you've seen with some of the other labs making their announcements, but that doesn't necessarily mean that they're all going to make their detectors publicly available.

146
00:14:33,344 --> 00:14:39,024
and even if you've got a publicly available detector, that can be motivation for people learning how to get around it.

147
00:14:39,104 --> 00:14:52,384
In the same way that some of these machine learning classifying-based detection systems are using a whole bunch of examples of text of things that have been watermarked, were a detector publicly available, and you could just query it over and over and over again.

148
00:14:52,464 --> 00:14:59,984
you might be able to work back to making something that gets around that specific configuration of keys that is presented in the public detector.

149
00:15:00,304 --> 00:15:00,624
Got you.

150
00:15:00,784 --> 00:15:07,264
So we're not at sort of guaranteed cast iron way to detect any AI generation whatsoever.

151
00:15:07,424 --> 00:15:10,144
It's just where this is a big step towards that.

152
00:15:10,544 --> 00:15:16,544
At least not with the method of having a publicly available detection system for every version of keys that is made publicly available.

153
00:15:16,846 --> 00:15:21,344
Aaron Powell Just in terms of images then and video, is it the same idea?

154
00:15:21,518 --> 00:15:23,904
You have a lot more space to hide things as you describe.

155
00:15:24,304 --> 00:15:24,516
Yes.

156
00:15:24,624 --> 00:15:32,624
So I think in images, the approach is different from the text watermarking approach that I was mentioning.

157
00:15:32,878 --> 00:15:49,924
In images, what happens is we take the image which has not been watermarked, and then there is a neural network which looks at that image and modifies it in a very subtle way to incorporate the signal.

158
00:15:50,542 --> 00:15:59,104
And this particular model, which is incorporating the watermark, is being co trained with a detector neural network.

159
00:16:00,622 --> 00:16:04,036
And they're both sort of trying to achieve the following.

160
00:16:04,528 --> 00:16:11,652
You get an image which is on watermark, the watermark generator injects a very small signal which is imperceptible.

161
00:16:12,206 --> 00:16:14,928
So if it is perceptible, we say that's not great.

162
00:16:15,088 --> 00:16:19,172
Like the users will see it, and that degrades the quality of the image.

163
00:16:19,568 --> 00:16:26,788
Then in the middle, there is an adversarial agent which is going to try to make modifications to the image.

164
00:16:28,208 --> 00:16:39,140
Might try to shrink the image, zoom in, crop it, rotate it, do many kinds of transformations like add noise and so on.

165
00:16:39,342 --> 00:16:45,892
And then the detector has to still detect whether the original image that is coming through is watermarked or not.

166
00:16:46,448 --> 00:16:46,768
Okay.

167
00:16:47,088 --> 00:16:56,928
And both of them are trained in tandem to make sure that regardless of what the adversary in the middle has done, the watermarks is still detectable.

168
00:16:57,390 --> 00:16:58,928
What does this mean for users then?

169
00:16:59,488 --> 00:17:06,608
I think the main thing is it's not just about text, it's about all these different modalities.

170
00:17:06,688 --> 00:17:21,248
And if you look at how people are using SynthID, like it was really amazing, the last few years as uh SynthID has been launched, I would open my favorite news website.

171
00:17:21,488 --> 00:17:23,887
and the reporter would say, "Well.

172
00:17:24,048 --> 00:17:41,728
here's an image that was circulating in the media about this particular house that magically was untouched in the California fires, or this image that came out from the Iran War or some other sort of topic.

173
00:17:41,968 --> 00:17:47,012
And we have run synth ID on it and detected that this was AI generated.

174
00:17:47,568 --> 00:17:48,208
Right.

175
00:17:48,608 --> 00:17:56,288
So all of a sudden, yeah, all of a sudden you can see the AI in places that I mean it definitely shouldn't be, basically.

176
00:17:56,768 --> 00:17:56,928
Yes.

177
00:17:57,166 --> 00:18:05,796
And so and and synth ID being used to actually tackle misinformation in real time and at scale.

178
00:18:06,574 --> 00:18:09,328
So then can anyone do what you just did there?

179
00:18:09,488 --> 00:18:16,368
Can anyone take an image, some text, some video, run it through synth ID, and find out whether it was generated by an AI model?

180
00:18:16,928 --> 00:18:22,928
we have made the detection ability embedded in the Gemini app, for instance.

181
00:18:23,008 --> 00:18:38,500
So today, if you can go to Gemini, if you ask, I'm looking at this image, tell me if it is AI generated, it can run the synth ID detector and say, yes, this is something that was generated by a Google image model and so on.

182
00:18:38,688 --> 00:18:48,368
And similar sort of abilities now are available by other companies as well who have adopted our models.

183
00:18:48,688 --> 00:18:52,208
Can each model only detect whether it was generated by that model?

184
00:18:52,368 --> 00:18:55,008
Can Gemini only detect Gemini generated content?

185
00:18:55,328 --> 00:19:02,212
So people who are injecting the watermark into the content, they have to agree to share the key.

186
00:19:02,768 --> 00:19:03,008
Right?

187
00:19:03,168 --> 00:19:06,528
If they share the keys, then we have the ability to detect it, right?

188
00:19:06,688 --> 00:19:08,928
So there can be a central service.

189
00:19:09,230 --> 00:19:24,128
If you go to the synth ID portal and if you ask something is uh generated by uh is AI generated, it can look at all the keys that have been given to us by partner institutions, and we can detect the AI content for all those partners as well.

190
00:19:24,448 --> 00:19:25,168
Okay, got you.

191
00:19:25,422 --> 00:19:28,288
But if a certain lab is like doesn't want to share their key, then you wouldn't be able to.

192
00:19:28,528 --> 00:19:28,688
Yeah.

193
00:19:28,910 --> 00:19:39,568
Okay, well, Jeremy, let's talk about biology now because I know that you've come up with this proof of concept system called synth ID Bio, which instead of watermarking you know text and audio and video.

