00:23:08,366 --> 00:23:19,808
And if something matches, then they either just block the order and say we can't synthesize this, or they'll go back to the original individual and try to find out what motivation they have for ordering it and whether they've got, say, requisite licensing.

233
00:23:19,918 --> 00:23:33,984
And the risk we have with these AI systems, as shown by some other academics, Is that you can make something that in sequence space is very different than something that's present in that pathogen database, but might fold into the thing that is of concern.

234
00:23:36,624 --> 00:23:36,868
Yep.

235
00:23:37,326 --> 00:23:40,384
But once it's created an actual protein, could be really harmful.

236
00:23:40,624 --> 00:23:45,104
Yeah, and it could have the same behavior as something that's present in the database of restricted environments.

237
00:23:45,344 --> 00:23:47,664
And their current systems wouldn't be able to catch it.

238
00:23:48,144 --> 00:23:56,944
definitely the concern is that and especially as these systems get better and better, you might be able to get something even further away in sequence space that might get around some of the existing protections.

239
00:23:57,424 --> 00:24:02,464
Something really wacky in this ribbon of amino acids that still can fold into something harmful.

240
00:24:02,624 --> 00:24:02,784
Yeah.

241
00:24:02,958 --> 00:24:06,784
To like to basically sneak under their detection system.

242
00:24:07,024 --> 00:24:07,264
Exactly.

243
00:24:07,344 --> 00:24:09,504
And you can cut and paste that into another sequence.

244
00:24:09,824 --> 00:24:20,388
So you can imagine, like, if maybe there's one portion of an order that would be blocked, you could cut and paste something of concern into that that's very far away from the restricted database.

245
00:24:20,686 --> 00:24:24,544
And then in the laboratory yourself, you just put it back into where it originally came from.

246
00:24:24,864 --> 00:24:26,224
Okay, I see.

