00:25:41,984 --> 00:25:43,104
Because that's an important point, right?

261
00:25:43,184 --> 00:25:51,984
That you might have these extremely complex folded structures, but that each protein is constructed from like a ribbon of amino acids, a string of amino acids.

262
00:25:52,064 --> 00:25:54,944
And that's where the analogy comes from, a string of amino acids like a string of text.

263
00:25:55,184 --> 00:25:55,344
Yep.

264
00:25:55,664 --> 00:26:07,172
And so what you almost see is that when you're folding a protein, there are locations where there can be swaps between individual amino acids, maybe ones that are similar in certain properties, like leucine and isoleucine are two that are quite similar.

265
00:26:07,374 --> 00:26:19,104
And so what we're taking advantage of within SynthID biosequence is that you can introduce watermarks through selections of individual amino acids that are likely to have both maintain the same structure and therefore have similar function.

266
00:26:19,424 --> 00:26:20,784
But hang on, how can you be sure?

267
00:26:20,944 --> 00:26:25,104
I mean, you said that you can make the swap and it doesn't matter, but can you be sure that the swap doesn't matter?

268
00:26:25,664 --> 00:26:25,744
Yeah.

269
00:26:25,984 --> 00:26:29,264
So that's what we specifically test in the process.

270
00:26:29,870 --> 00:26:49,824
So, the whole idea of this research was to inject the watermark and ensure that the generated sequence, amino acid sequence in this case, that builds the protein has the same function or retains the function that we were trying to design for.

271
00:26:50,638 --> 00:26:57,732
And that's what is very cool about it that we have now in existence watermarked proteins.

272
00:26:58,064 --> 00:26:59,024
These are real things.

273
00:26:59,104 --> 00:27:03,664
This is not in simulation, these are real things which do stuff.

274
00:27:04,590 --> 00:27:07,460
But which are watermarked and will always be detectable.

275
00:27:08,238 --> 00:27:12,676
And importantly, from our own laboratory experiments, they don't change the function at all.

276
00:27:12,944 --> 00:27:13,984
So we made protein binders.

277
00:27:14,064 --> 00:27:14,784
So we made the things that stick.

278
00:27:15,024 --> 00:27:17,892
Oh, you physically made them and we tested them in a laboratory.

279
00:27:18,064 --> 00:27:22,464
And some of the measurements you can get from making protein binders are things like hit rates.

280
00:27:22,544 --> 00:27:27,744
So of the number of designs that you send in the lab, how many of them actually bind, can look for how well something binds.

281
00:27:27,950 --> 00:27:39,424
And across both those measures, things that were watermarked and watermarked under two different schemes versus protein binders that weren't watermarked had near identical hit rates, near-identical quantitative measures of binding.

282
00:27:40,220 --> 00:27:40,900
Because you'd expect to

283
00:27:40,900 --> 00:27:42,704
lose maybe a tiny bit, right?

284
00:27:43,150 --> 00:27:43,504
Potentially.

285
00:27:43,664 --> 00:27:48,944
I mean, I guess admittedly, I think before we got the wet lab data, I was a little bit skeptical about how well this is gonna work.

286
00:27:49,024 --> 00:27:51,984
And I think it blew all of our minds actually just how easy it was.

287
00:27:52,144 --> 00:27:56,580
But proving it in biology was amazing, like I think something that took us all maybe a little bit by surprise.

288
00:27:57,198 --> 00:27:59,264
I know this is proof of concept at the moment.

289
00:27:59,344 --> 00:28:01,700
You're sort of demonstrating that it works and that it's possible.

290
00:28:02,144 --> 00:28:07,744
Is the idea that in the future any lab would be able to read your watermark?

291
00:28:08,174 --> 00:28:09,264
Yeah, and I think that's the eventual goal.

292
00:28:09,344 --> 00:28:11,744
Or maybe I wouldn't even necessarily frame it as our watermark.

293
00:28:11,824 --> 00:28:13,504
Maybe it's the technology other people can use.

294
00:28:13,664 --> 00:28:18,276
We know that there has to be a number of different players who would be part of actually operationalizing this.

295
00:28:18,414 --> 00:28:28,864
So, model developers, people making either novel tools or systems to be able to generate new proteins, they're going to have to be sort of on board of being able to integrate watermarking within their own schemes.

296
00:28:28,944 --> 00:28:34,052
And because we're open sourcing the code, this is something that they can do without even necessarily needing to coordinate with us.

297
00:28:34,350 --> 00:28:45,664
And in the synthesis side, there'll have to be some ability to exchange keys and exchange other details about the watermarking between the model providers and the DNA synthesis company themselves.

298
00:28:45,744 --> 00:28:48,144
And so these are a couple of different coordinations that need to happen.

299
00:28:48,398 --> 00:28:56,384
From our side, we know that there is additional engineering headroom that you can move into to make the watermark both detectable and potentially a little bit easier to embed.

300
00:28:56,464 --> 00:28:57,860
We know that from some of the ongoing work.

301
00:28:58,544 --> 00:29:06,372
and this is the type of activities that we would need to see within the community to be able to make this easier to adopt from all the different players who are involved.

302
00:29:06,704 --> 00:29:08,384
Is it the same story with the text?

303
00:29:08,544 --> 00:29:16,304
Would this be able to detect anything universally, or is it specific to the particular key that's used?

304
00:29:16,590 --> 00:29:29,984
So if someone were to take the implementation that we're making publicly available and integrate it in their system, so long as they're able to share the key as well as the length of context, that's another area that you can change how much of the preceding context matters for watermarking.

305
00:29:30,064 --> 00:29:33,984
So long as you can share those two values, you'd be able to detect them coming out of any system.

306
00:29:34,224 --> 00:29:36,384
Is this out there working already?

307
00:29:37,024 --> 00:29:38,464
what stage are you at right now?

308
00:29:38,704 --> 00:29:46,304
So for us, what we've been able to do within this publication is more or less have a proof of concept that we're going to open source the technology that allows people to do the watermarking.

309
00:29:46,574 --> 00:29:53,104
So this is a slightly different one than the way that we can watermark content coming out of Google, as we don't have necessarily protein design service internally.

310
00:29:53,264 --> 00:30:01,104
So what we've started to do is have a lot of conversations with other people in the field and sort of in the ecosystem, both on the model development side as well as on the synthesis screening side.

