00:31:10,576 --> 00:31:26,896
And so, they have been extremely receptive of the technology because they see the challenges every day that they are getting these orders for gene synthesis, which can create proteins.

323
00:31:27,056 --> 00:31:34,628
and they have these filters that can catch problematic orders, but they know that those are not imperfect.

324
00:31:35,502 --> 00:31:46,256
and more work needs to be done to develop them, and they want to work with the field and their community to find solutions that will work.

325
00:31:46,656 --> 00:31:53,412
Because this this topic of bioresilience as we go forwards is going to be, I mean, it's really going to start to become more and more on people's minds.

326
00:31:53,966 --> 00:31:56,496
I mean, what are the other things that you're doing in this area?

327
00:31:56,910 --> 00:32:05,028
There was a memo that came out, I think it was two months ago or so, that sort of laid out Google DeepMind's vision as a whole, plus isomorphic labs of where we're thinking about bioresilience.

328
00:32:05,376 --> 00:32:12,816
And that matched a couple different efforts across pillars that are actually rooted in the UK's biological security strategy, so on prevent, detect, and respond.

329
00:32:13,376 --> 00:32:15,456
our team has a couple different efforts under that.

330
00:32:15,536 --> 00:32:20,036
And broadly, we're looking at how we can leverage AI to reduce biological risk.

331
00:32:20,416 --> 00:32:24,676
It's focused both on natural threats and on sort of AI-enhanced augmented threats.

332
00:32:25,038 --> 00:32:27,536
We've got some projects that map really specifically to one area.

333
00:32:27,616 --> 00:32:35,428
So I think this synthetic bio is a good example where we're really thinking about AI-enhanced AI designs and what that might mean for being able to do pathogen detection.

334
00:32:36,174 --> 00:32:39,908
We've got some other concepts that are much more in the natural threat space, and then some that sort of map between the both.

335
00:32:40,096 --> 00:32:49,136
So, sort of our theory of change is that if you make biology less useful for someone who's trying to do something malicious, then that in and of itself can prevent them from using it in the first case.

336
00:32:49,536 --> 00:33:02,016
So, for us, if we're able to make new capabilities and tools that make our entire public health system more robust or make it easier for epidemiologists to identify an outbreak, that can in and of itself make an outbreak less likely.

337
00:33:02,416 --> 00:33:04,496
Because you want the good side of this, right?

338
00:33:04,656 --> 00:33:09,136
You want like the benefits of it, but you don't want it to come along with all of the potential downsides.

339
00:33:09,536 --> 00:33:10,496
Absolutely, absolutely.

340
00:33:10,656 --> 00:33:20,932
And I think AI will have immense positive impacts in human health and understanding of biology at large.

341
00:33:21,536 --> 00:33:24,976
And I mean, it's already happening if like around us, right?

342
00:33:25,166 --> 00:33:31,620
Isomorphic labs and many other sort of companies who are leveraging AI for drug discovery, understanding human health and so on.

343
00:33:32,176 --> 00:33:48,260
But at the same time, there will be problematic use cases that will be enabled, and we need all the controls and all the sort of measures that we can place to make sure that those tasks are difficult or become infeasible.

344
00:33:48,910 --> 00:33:55,616
Just going back to the landscape of digital media for a moment, because I mean at the moment I think this stuff is quite fragmented.

345
00:33:56,046 --> 00:34:04,016
Do you think that we will get to a point where there is a central space that you can go to that will just tell you whether something is AI generated or not?

346
00:34:04,336 --> 00:34:07,172
I think the field is moving towards that.

347
00:34:07,376 --> 00:34:30,724
There is overall sort of industry alignment on the need for provenance solutions, where companies have agreed on a standard of, uh, uh, of like, uh, making sure that if, when there are edits or when there is generative AI, like sort of placing that information alongside the file.

348
00:34:32,335 --> 00:34:40,900
the issue with that is that metadata can be sort of stripped off, which is why we need strong binding, which what watermarks provide.

349
00:34:41,295 --> 00:34:48,976
So by combining these approaches and by building a solution that works for everyone.

350
00:34:50,032 --> 00:34:50,911
in the community.

351
00:34:50,992 --> 00:34:53,860
I think we'll be able to get to a standard.

352
00:34:54,926 --> 00:34:58,820
But I think like progress is being made on that, but it just takes time.

353
00:34:59,438 --> 00:35:04,352
But this also, I guess, same question for the biological side of things.

354
00:35:04,592 --> 00:35:09,392
Is that the aim then that there will be this industry standard at some point that everybody is using?

355
00:35:09,712 --> 00:35:18,564
There's a lot of things, and we even know this in the discussion where we know that there's engineering headroom to be able to move into, both in the way that you're calculating g-values and introducing the watermark.

356
00:35:19,502 --> 00:35:32,272
but at its surface, we do sort of believe that watermarking embedded in an imperceptible way within the sequences themselves is likely to be one of the best ways forward for being able to identify the provenance of AI-generated sequences.

357
00:35:32,592 --> 00:35:36,112
I do also wonder though, whether there's a bit of a game of cat and mouse going on here, right?

358
00:35:36,272 --> 00:35:44,644
Because the more universal a system becomes, the more sort of that it's like it's clear what the rules are and how it's implemented.

359
00:35:45,070 --> 00:35:49,472
Does it not become easier for somebody to come along with another system that is able to undo it?

360
00:35:49,712 --> 00:36:06,032
Yes, so I think this is very much a risk, which is why I think it's really important for us to have these measures where you have the secret keys with which we can embed watermarks in the signal itself, right?

361
00:36:06,112 --> 00:36:14,116
Whether it's a protein sequence, whether it's images and so on, and which are hard to take away.

362
00:36:14,446 --> 00:36:23,632
Like and it's and like the ideal situation will be hard to take away to an extent that if you want to get rid of the detection signal that you have to change the function, right?

363
00:36:23,792 --> 00:36:30,352
And destroy the original sort of capability of the task that you had designed the thing for.

364
00:36:30,752 --> 00:36:33,712
Like it's so embedded within it that there's no way to undo it.

365
00:36:33,952 --> 00:36:34,112
Yes.

366
00:36:34,272 --> 00:36:38,752
But I guess it is as you said, this is not necessarily the final way of doing things.

367
00:36:38,912 --> 00:36:45,872
This is sort of early days, the fact that you can have like a sort of biological object, right?

368
00:36:45,952 --> 00:36:51,232
Like a sort of right down at that level that contains within it the story of where it came from.

369
00:36:51,472 --> 00:36:52,512
Yeah, it's pretty incredible.

370
00:36:52,592 --> 00:37:03,460
And I guess I would say we're not the first ones to do watermarks, but we're the first ones to our knowledge to both show imperceptibility and function preservation, and including in vitro and sort of lab-based results.

